%[text] # **Color Segmentation with Thresholds and Distance**.
%[text] 
%[text] InIn the previous live script, we used the L\*a\*b\* color model to identify red strawberries against a mostly green background. That worked because we were looking for one dominant color.
%[text] But what happens when an image contains **several different colors**, like this image of M&Ms?
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:051d)
%[text] 
%[text] In this image we have six candy colors plus the light-brown table. A single threshold will not be enough.
%[text] L\*a\*b\* gives us two color coordinates:
%[text] - a\*: approximately **green ↔ red/magenta**
%[text] - b\*: approximately **blue ↔ yellow** \
%[text] We will try two different strategies.
%[text] **Method 1: Treat** **a\*** **and** **b\*** **independently**
%[text] Divide each color channel into ranges, create a mask from each channel, and combine the masks.
%[text] **Method 2: Treat color as a location in** **a\*b\*** **space**
%[text] Represent every pixel using both its a\* and b\* coordinates at the same time, then group pixels that are close together in this two-dimensional color space.
%[text] The second method will lead us to **k-means clustering**.
%%
%[text] ## Learning goals
%[text] By the end of this live script, you should be able to:
%[text] - use multithresh to divide one color channel into several value ranges,
%[text] - use imquantize to convert those ranges into class labels,
%[text] - explain the difference between a **class-label image** and an **object-label image**,
%[text] - combine masks created from the a\* and b\* channels,
%[text] - explain why treating a\* and b\* independently has limitations,
%[text] - visualize pixels as points in the two-dimensional a\*b\* color plane,
%[text] - use imsegkmeans to cluster pixels according to color,
%[text] - convert color clusters into a binary object mask, and
%[text] - identify the dominant color cluster inside each segmented object. \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_D9896EFC] ## Method 1: Multilevel thresholding
%[text] ### Load Image
%[text] Let's start by loading the M&M image.
close all % close all figures
clearvars % clear workspace
url = 'https://blogs.mathworks.com/images/steve/2010/mms.jpg'; 
p.name = 'mms.jpg';
p.rgb = imread(url);
figure;
imshow(p.rgb)
%[text] #### **Before moving on**
%[text] Look at the image and identify the segmentation problem.
%[text] - How many candy colors do you see?
%[text] - Is the table perfectly uniform in color?
%[text] - Would one threshold be enough to separate every candy from the table? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7D545356] ### **Convert image to L\*a\*b\* space** and review histogram
%[text] First, we convert the RGB image  L\*a\*b space using the function **rgb2lab.**
p.lab = rgb2lab(p.rgb);
%[text] Remember that the three L\*a\*b\* channels contain different kinds of information:
%[text] - L\* describes **lightness**.
%[text] - a\* describes position along the **green-to-red/magenta** axis.
%[text] - b\* describes position along the **blue-to-yellow** axis. \
%[text] Let's display each channel and its histogram.

figure
tiledlayout(2,3,"TileSpacing","tight")
p.titles = ["L (lightness)", " a (green-red)", "b (yellow-blue)"];
for n=1:3
    nexttile(n);
    imshow(p.lab(:,:,n),[]) % index out the channel and display
    title(p.titles(n));

    nexttile(n+3)
    histogram(p.lab(:,:,n))
end
%[text] **Important:** The empty brackets in imshow(A,\[\]) rescale the values **for display only**. They do not normalize or change the values stored in A.
%[text] #### **What should you notice?**
%[text] - The L\* image resembles a grayscale image because it contains the lightness information.
%[text] - Shadows and highlights are especially visible in L\*.
%[text] - The interiors of many candies look relatively uniform in the a\* and b\* channels.
%[text] - Some candy colors separate more clearly in a\*, while others separate more clearly in b\*.
%[text] - The a\* and b\* histograms contain several groups of values because the image contains several different colors. \
%[text] Since our goal is primarily to separate **colors**, we will first work with a\* and b\* and ignore L\*.
%%
%[text] ### Examine the a\* channel
%[text] Index out the a\* channel and store it in a structure named a.
a.name = "M&Ms a plane";
a.plane = p.lab(:,:,2);

figure 
histogram(a.plane, 50);
title('a* plane')
grid minor
%[text] The histogram shows how frequently different a\* values occur in the image.
%[text] A single threshold would divide this histogram into only two groups. Here, however, we can see several groups of values.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C547FC48] ### Multi-level thresholding 
%[text] Previously, we used one threshold to divide pixels into two groups. Here we want several groups.
%[text] multithresh calculates several thresholds automatically. Here we ask MATLAB for **three thresholds**.
a.thresh = multithresh(a.plane,3)
%[text] a.thresh is a 1x3 vector containing the three threshold values.
%[text] Three thresholds divide the data into **four value ranges**:
%[text] ```
%[text] range 1 | threshold 1 | range 2 | threshold 2 | range 3 | threshold 3 | range 4
%[text] ```
%[text] The exact numerical thresholds are less important than this relationship: **N thresholds create N + 1 value ranges.**
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D7A77761] ### Visualizing the Multiple Thresholds
%[text] Plot the a\* histogram again and add the thresholds as vertical lines.
figure
hh = histogram(a.plane, 50);
xline(a.thresh,'r--')
title('Multi-level thresholding for a*plane')
grid minor
%[text] - The three vertical lines divide the a\* values into four classes. \
%[text] At this stage, MATLAB has divided **channel values** into groups. It has not identified individual candies.
%[text] That distinction becomes important in the next step.
%%
%[text] ### Convert the value ranges into class labels
%[text] Use imquantize to assign each pixel to one of the four a\* ranges.
a.label = imquantize(a.plane,a.thresh);

fprintf("\na.label:\n - min: %d\n - max: %d\n", ...
    min(a.label(:)),max(a.label(:)))
%[text] a.label contains the integers 1, 2, 3, and 4.
%[text] Each integer tells us **which** **a\*** **value range** that pixel belongs to.
%[text] - label 1 = pixels in the lowest a\* range,
%[text] - label 2 = pixels in the next a\* range,
%[text] - label 3 = pixels in the next a\* range,
%[text] - label 4 = pixels in the highest a\* range. \
%[text] Pixels with the same class label do **not** have to touch one another.
%[text] **Question**
%[text] If two candies are far apart but have similar a\* values, could they receive the same class label?
%[text] **Yes.** The label describes a range of color-channel values, not an individual physical object.
%%
%[text] ### Display the class-label image
%[text] Here we display the label matrix and use a parula look up table with just 4 colors
figure
imshow(a.label,parula(4))

ticks = 1:max(a.label(:));
colorbar("Ticks",ticks,"TickLabels",ticks)

title("a* Class Labels")
impixelinfo
%[text] The colors used in this display come from the parula lookup table.
%[text] A green-looking class in this figure does **not** mean that those pixels are physically green in the original image. The display colors are only used to help us distinguish the numeric labels.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C5BC0A9E] ### Find the Background Class
%[text] In **this particular image**, the table occupies more pixels than any other a\* class. We can use that fact as a simple way to identify the background. This is a **heuristic**. **Heuristic:** a convenient rule that works because of something we know about a particular problem. It is not guaranteed to work for every image.
%[text] regionprops can measure the total area associated with each numeric label, even if pixels with that label occur in disconnected locations.
rpA = regionprops('table',a.label,'area')
%[text] The rows correspond to labels 1, 2, 3, and 4.
%[text] Find the label with the largest total area.
[~,a.background] = max(rpA.Area)
%[text] #### **Check your result**
%[text] - Which class was identified as the background?
%[text] - Does that agree with the class-label image?
%[text] - Why might this strategy fail on a different image? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0AD0E639] ### Convert the class-label image to a binary mask
%[text] We want the candies to be true and the background to be false.
figure;
a.mask=a.label~=a.background; % Keep every pixel whose class label is **not equal** to the background label.
imshow(a.mask)
title("Mask from the a* Channel")
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_69F369D7] ### Clean the a\* mask
%[text] Remove small regions of noise and close small gaps.
a.mask = bwareaopen(a.mask,250);

se = strel("diamond",1);
a.mask = imclose(a.mask,se);

figure
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,a.mask)
title("Candies Found Using a*")
%[text] The a\* channel capture most candy colors, except for blue and brown.  
%[text] That is not surprising. We are trying to describe color using only **one coordinate**.
%%
%[text] ### Repeat the process for the b\* channel
%[text] The following local function recreates the step from above, but for the b plane
b = get_plane_mask(p.lab(:,:,3),"M&Ms b plane")

figure
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,b.mask)
title("Candies Found Using b*")
%[text] - Ok, not bad either
%[text] - Now we found most candies, but the red ones \
%%
%[text] #### Review: Why would the a\* and b\* masks find different candies?
%[text] Because each channel describes a different color direction. A color that blends into the table along one axis may be much easier to distinguish along the other.
%%
%[text] ### Combine the two masks
%[text] If we overlay the two masks, we see that collectively they capture all the candies.
imshowpair(a.mask,b.mask)
title("a* Mask Compared with b* Mask")
%[text] - So, if we combine the two masks, then we get all the candies.  \
%[text] ### 
%[text] ### Optional: Slightly expand the mask
%[text] If the mask is slightly smaller than the visible candies, we can expand it by one pixel using dilation.
se = strel("diamond",1);
p.abMask = imdilate(p.abMask,se);

figure
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,p.abMask)
title("Dilated Mask")
%[text] Dilation does not recover the true candy boundary. It simply adds foreground pixels around the current mask.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_59F4E4EC] ### Separate touching candies
%[text] Here we use the `mmGetWatershed` function to separate the touching candies
p.amask = mmGetWatershed(p.amask,5,ShowSteps=true)
%[text] 
%[text] ### Method 1: Why does this work?
%[text] Some candy colors separate well along the a\* axis, while others separate well along the b\* axis. Combining the masks lets either channel contribute pixels.
%[text] **What is the limitation?**
%[text] We are still treating a\* and b\* **independently**.
%[text] A color, however, is described by the combination of both coordinates.
%[text] That suggests a different idea:
%[text] Instead of asking where a pixel falls along one axis at a time, what if we ask where it falls in the entire a\*b\* plane?
%[text] That is the idea behind our second method.
%%
%[text] ## Method 2 — K-means Clustering in the a\*b\* Plane
%[text] The first method treated the two color channels independently. Now we will use them **together**.
%[text] Each pixel has two color coordinates: $\\\[ (a^\*,b^\*) \\\]$
%[text] That means every pixel can be represented as a point in a two-dimensional coordinate system. Pixels with similar colors should appear near one another in this a\*b\* plane.
%%
%[text] ### Visualize the a\*b\* color space
%[text] Extract the two color channels.
p.ab = p.lab(:,:,2:3);
%[text] Reshape the image so that each row contains the a\* and b\* coordinates of one pixel.
abPoints = reshape(p.ab,[],2);
%[text] There are many pixels, so plot only every 20th pixel to keep the figure manageable.
sample = 1:20:size(abPoints,1);

figure
scatter(abPoints(sample,1),abPoints(sample,2),6,"filled")
xlabel("a*")
ylabel("b*")
title("Pixels in a*b* Color Space")
grid on
axis equal
%[text] - Instead of a one-dimensional histogram, we can now see groups of pixels in **two dimensions**. \
%[text] If groups of similarly colored pixels form clusters in this plot, how could we ask the computer to separate those groups automatically?
%[text] One answer is **k-means clustering**.
%%
%[text] ### Optional: Use Color thresholder to view the colors in L\*a\*b\* space
colorThresholder(p.rgb)
%%
%[text] ### How k-means works
%[text] K-means is a clustering algorithm.
%[text] ![](text:image:9f8b)
%[text] We first tell it how many groups, or **clusters**, we want. K-means then repeatedly:
%[text] 1. places a center for each cluster,
%[text] 2. assigns pixels to the nearest center,
%[text] 3. moves each center toward the pixels assigned to it,
%[text] 4. repeats the process until the assignments stabilize. \
%[text] Here, distance is measured using the a\* and b\* coordinates.
%[text] So pixels assigned to the same cluster should have similar colors.
%[text] #### One important limitation
%[text] K-means does **not** determine the number of clusters for us. We must choose K in advance.
%[text] There are six visible candy colors, but the table is not perfectly uniform. Lighting and shading can cause the table pixels to occupy more than one cluster. For this example, we will try **eight clusters**.
%[text] This is a **modeling choice**, not a number that k-means discovered automatically.
%[text] ### Segmenting with k-means
%[text] The image processing tool box has a function `imsegkmeans` that allows you to sort color values using k-means clustering.
%[text] The steps are as follows
p.ab = im2single(p.ab); % type-cast to a single (for input into imsegkmeans
nColors = 8; % Predetermined number of colors
p.kLabel = imsegkmeans(p.ab,nColors, 'NumAttempts',3); % use K-means clustering
%[text] - NumAttempts=3 tells MATLAB to try several initial cluster-center locations and keep the best result.
%[text] - p.kLabel is a **class-label image,** just like we created using `imquantize`
%[text] - Each number identifies a **color cluster**, not an individual candy. \
%%
%[text] ### Visualize the k-means labels
figure

tiledlayout(1,2,"TileSpacing","none","Padding","tight")

ax(1) = nexttile;
imshow(p.rgb)
title("Original Image")

ax(2) = nexttile;
imshow(p.kLabel,turbo(nColors))
title("K-means Cluster Labels")

clim([0.5 nColors+0.5])
cb = colorbar;
cb.Ticks = 1:nColors;

impixelinfo
linkaxes(ax,"xy")
%[text] - And it works amazing well.
%[text] - Each **candy color** is largely assigned to a distinct k-means cluster.
%[text] - The table is divided into more than one cluster because its appearance varies across the image. \
%%
%[text] ### Cluster numbers are arbitrary
%[text] K-means may assign numbers such as 1, 2, or 7 to different clusters.
%[text] Those numbers do **not** have built-in meanings.
%[text] Cluster 4 does not inherently mean "yellow." It simply means the pixels assigned to cluster number 4 in this clustering result.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### Identifying the background clusters labels
%[text] For this particular image, the table occupies most of the image. Because the table is split across two large color clusters, the two clusters with the greatest total area are good candidates for the background.
%[text] Again, this is an **image-specific heuristic**.
rp = regionprops('table',p.kLabel,'Area');

[~,idx] = maxk(rp.Area,2)   % two largest color clusters
%[text] Be sure to confirm the labels found, 1 and 8, are the table label. Always calculate then visually validate
%[text]  The rest of the labels are for the colors found in the different candies. 
%[text] 
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### Create a binary candy mask
%[text] Now we know which clusters do not represent the clusters found in the candies. If we exclude those labels, we can create a logical array for all the other cluster labels found in the label map
p.kMask = ~ismember(p.kLabel,idx); % creates a logical array of labels that don't match the 2 largest labels
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,p.kMask)
%[text] - ismember asks whether each cluster label belongs to the list of background labels.
%[text] - The ~ reverses the result, so the background clusters become false and the remaining clusters become true. \
%[text] And we're done. One stop shopping for M&M labels
%%
%[text] ### Clean the k-means mask
p.kMask = imfill(p.kMask,'holes'); 
p.kMask = bwareaopen(p.kMask,200); % clean up small noise
p.kMask = bwmorph(p.kMask,'thicken');
p.kMask = mmGetWatershed(p.kMask,5); % separate touching candies
mmShowBurnImage(p.rgb,p.kMask)
title("Cleaned K-means Mask")
%%
%[text] ### Determine the dominant color cluster inside each candy
%[text] We now have two different images:
%[text] - p.kMask tells us **where the candies are**.
%[text] - p.kLabel tells us **which color cluster each pixel belongs to**. \
%[text] We can combine those two sources of information. Ideally, most pixels inside one candy should have the same cluster label. In practice, reflections, shadows, edges, and segmentation noise may cause a few pixels inside the candy to have different labels.
%[text] For that reason, we will use the **mode**, or most common label, inside each candy.
rp = regionprops('table',p.kMask,p.kLabel,["Centroid","Area","PixelValues"])
%[text] - PixelValues contains the k-means labels found inside each connected candy.
%[text] - It is stored as a cell array because every candy contains a different number of pixels. \
%%
%[text] The function `cellfun` is like a FOR LOOP for cell arrays. With one simple function call, it unpackages each cell in the array and then applies the indicated function. Here we apply the mode. The @ symbol is just the handle the function (it's name). And then add the output back to `rp`
rp.Label = cellfun(@mode,rp.PixelValues) % calculate the mode for each candy Pixel values
%[text] - Now rp.Label contains the dominant k-means cluster for every candy. \
%%
%[text] ### Display the cluster number on each candy
%[text] Here we label each candy with its label from the label map
figure
imshow(p.rgb)
for n=1:height(rp)
    x = rp.Centroid(n,1);
    y = rp.Centroid(n,2);
    text(x,y,sprintf('%d',rp.Label(n)),'Color','w')
end
title("Label")
%[text] - Candies with the same color should generally have the same cluster number. \
%%
%[text] ### Pairing Cluster label to color
%[text] K-means does not know the words "blue," "green," or "orange." It only knows which pixels belong together.
%[text] You can inspect the labeled image and manually create a mapping between the cluster numbers and color names.
%[text] This only works for this example. In another M&M image, the label numbers would be different and we would have to manually label those numbers as well. 
%[text] But, let's just try it, for fun, using the function `categorical`
rp.Color = categorical(rp.Label,2:7,{'blue','green','orange','yellow','brown','red'})
%[text] Here we label each candy with its color name
figure
imshow(p.rgb)
for n=1:height(rp)
    x = rp.Centroid(n,1);
    y = rp.Centroid(n,2);
    text(x,y,sprintf('%s',rp.Color(n)),'Color','w',HorizontalAlignment='center')
end
title("Color Name")
%[text] - Now we have a visual confirmation that each color of M&M was labeled with the same k-means cluster label \
%%
%[text] # Compare the two methods
%[text:table]
%[text] | **Method** | **How it treats color** | **Strength** | **Limitation** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Multilevel thresholding | Separates a\* and b\* independently | Simple and easy to inspect | Ignores the joint relationship between the two color coordinates |
%[text] | K-means | Treats each pixel as a point in a\*b\* space | Uses both color coordinates at the same time | Requires choosing the number of clusters and produces arbitrary cluster numbers |
%[text:table]
%[text] **Thresholding asks:**
%[text] Where does this pixel fall along one axis?
%[text] **K-means asks:**
%[text] Which cluster center is this pixel closest to in two-dimensional color space?
%[text] That is why k-means can be useful when several colors must be separated at the same time.
%%
%[text] ## Where do we go from here?
%[text] Color segmentation works when the object and background differ in color in a useful and measurable way. But what happens when color is unavailable or unreliable?
%[text] In the next exercise, we will look at **texture**: how rough or smooth a region is. We will use texture to find a flatfish camouflaged against sand, a case where color alone is not enough.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_511EB36B] # Local Functions
function s = get_plane_mask(plane,name)
s.name = name;
s.plane = plane;
s.thresh = multithresh(plane,3);
s.label = imquantize(plane,s.thresh);
rp = regionprops('table',s.label,'area');
[~,s.background] = max(rp.Area);
s.mask = s.label ~= s.background;
s.mask = bwareaopen(s.mask,250); % remove any small noise
se = strel('diamond', 1); % Create the structuring element
s.mask = imclose(s.mask, se); % Fill any  holes in the connected components
end




